WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.300
After seeing Alien: Covenant, you probably
have some questions.

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:07.479
That's okay — like Prometheus before it,
Alien: Covenant was long on terror, and short

00:00:07.479 --> 00:00:08.479
on answers.

00:00:08.479 --> 00:00:11.900
As such, we're here to do our best to get
to the bottom of some of these mysteries,

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:15.770
and try to figure out just what the takeaway
is from Ridley Scott's latest extraterrestrial

00:00:15.770 --> 00:00:16.780
murderfest.

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:21.210
So now, with a very obvious spoiler warning,
let's try to explain the ending of Alien:

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:22.210
Covenant.

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:23.880
Why did David help Daniels and Tennessee?

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.840
If you were surprised when the synthetic at
the end of the movie turned out to be the

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:31.290
evil, British David and not the kind, bland
Walter, then you haven't seen a lot of movies,

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:32.290
have you?

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:35.440
The minute you see Michael Fassbender playing
two robots — one good, one evil — you

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:39.010
have to know that the bad one's going to pull
the ol' dipsy-doo-switcheroo on our heroes

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:40.170
by the end of the movie.

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:41.589
It's basically movie law.

00:00:41.589 --> 00:00:45.920
However, even if the David reveal was somewhat
predictable, it does raise the question: why

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:49.890
did David help Daniels and Tennessee during
the final showdown with the xenomorph?

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:54.039
It's not completely clear, but it seems likely
that David suffered from a planet-sized case

00:00:54.039 --> 00:00:55.039
of hubris.

00:00:55.039 --> 00:00:58.799
His whole plan seems to center around finding
ways to make the xenomorph better through

00:00:58.799 --> 00:00:59.799
experimentation.

00:00:59.799 --> 00:01:02.949
We got a glimpse of some of his trials and
errors when we saw his lab — along with

00:01:02.949 --> 00:01:05.610
what remained of Elizabeth Shaw from Prometheus.

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:09.592
It's possible that the final showdown aboard
the Covenant was, for David, a test to see

00:01:09.592 --> 00:01:12.729
whether this version of the xenomorph could
get the better of Daniels and Tennessee.

00:01:12.729 --> 00:01:18.420
It failed, so he's got plans for bigger, badder
aliens when the ship finally arrives at Origae-6.

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:22.770
On the other hand, maybe he simply saw something
in Daniels that he wanted to preserve — again,

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:26.170
so he could use her in his future experiments.

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:27.340
Who's mommy?

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:30.700
At one point, David says that his alien pals
are "waiting for mother."

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:32.729
There are plenty of ways to interpret this
line.

00:01:32.729 --> 00:01:37.149
A pretty obvious reading is that the alien
eggs — and more specifically, the facehuggers

00:01:37.149 --> 00:01:40.270
within — need a host to do the bloody and
nasty with.

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:43.929
In essence, anyone who provides an incubation
chamber for a xenomorph is "mother."

00:01:43.929 --> 00:01:45.990
But what if there's more to it than that?

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:49.539
Prometheus and Alien: Covenant are prequels
to the original Alien franchise — and that

00:01:49.539 --> 00:01:52.759
includes Aliens, which was written and directed
by James Cameron.

00:01:52.759 --> 00:01:57.219
In that movie, we learned the source of all
those lovely alien eggs: the Alien Queen.

00:01:57.219 --> 00:02:00.600
Since we didn't get to see anything resembling
a xenomorph queen before leaving the Engineers'

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:03.400
planet, it's likely that it hasn't been created
yet.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:06.880
David can't stay up forever making new eggs,
so the queen's creation has to be on David's

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:07.880
agenda.

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:12.320
And that may spell a pretty bad morning for
our hero Daniels when she wakes up...

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:13.640
What did it all mean?

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:17.250
It may be tough to decipher, but it sure seems
like Ridley Scott was trying to tell us something

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:20.140
with his tale of space colonization gone horribly
awry.

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:21.860
Well, let's think about this.

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:25.230
Daniels and her poor, dead husband Jacob came
aboard the Covenant with hopes of starting

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:28.310
a new life on a new planet — just because
it's an adventure.

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:32.380
Meanwhile, poor, dead Captain Christopher
decided to stop on the Engineers' planet in

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:36.300
response to the weird communication they picked
up, and to see if this new world might be

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:38.860
even better than the one they'd been going
toward already.

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:42.440
And David decided that he should play god
and out-create his creators.

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:44.020
What was the result of all of these decisions?

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:45.900
Chaos, destruction, and death.

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:49.700
More than anything, Alien: Covenant is about
the dangers of unchecked human ambition.

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:52.950
If Daniels and her fella had just stayed on
Earth and rented a nice apartment somewhere,

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:55.450
he probably wouldn't have burnt to a crisp
in a hypersleep pod.

00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:59.940
And if Christopher hadn't diverted away from
Origae-6 — well, then we'd have had a movie

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:03.580
about the dangers of micromanaging a group
of drunk spaceship workers.

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:07.180
But there probably would've been far fewer
blood barfs and body bursts.

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:09.790
And if David hadn't decided to go full-on
supervillain?

00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:14.690
Well, then he wouldn't have inadvertently
become that which he despised.

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:15.890
What happens next?

00:03:15.890 --> 00:03:20.540
Two months before Alien: Covenant's May 2017
release, The Sydney Morning Herald reported

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:22.630
on a set visit with director Ridley Scott.

00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:25.570
In that article, Scott revealed that he's
already got the next movie in the prequel

00:03:25.570 --> 00:03:27.410
series written and ready to go.

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:30.650
That means that the dangling plot threads
of what'll happen when the Covenant arrives

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:34.740
at Origae-6 will be answered in whatever treatment
Scott's got locked in his desk drawer.

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:38.450
Presumably, we'll see a whole mess of xenomorphs
bursting out of colonists, not to mention

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:42.700
a final showdown with David and Daniels...or
whoever else signs on for a sequel if and

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:45.650
when 20th Century Fox announces it's happening.

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:47.700
What happens when the prequels are over?

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:51.580
Not only has Scott got the sequel to Covenant
ready to go, The Hollywood Reporter said that

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:55.060
Scott's revealed plans to make yet another
movie in his prequel series.

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:58.240
That fourth movie would apparently link up
with the events of the first Alien movie from

00:03:58.240 --> 00:03:59.240
1979.

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:02.580
And then, no more Alien movies ever, right?

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:03.580
Well…

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:07.110
The entire reason we're talking about new
Alien movies right now at all is that franchises

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:09.830
tend to go for as long as people can earn
money from them.

00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:13.850
Whatever your opinion on the current crop
of Alien flicks, making new movies in a recognizable

00:04:13.850 --> 00:04:16.430
series of movies is a sure way to earn cash.

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:19.591
So even though these prequels will have to
lead into the original movie somehow, there

00:04:19.591 --> 00:04:22.060
will likely be some effort to keep the brand
alive.

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:26.210
That might mean sequels that pick up after
the events of Alien: Resurrection — or,

00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:28.260
God help us, Alien vs. Predator.

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:31.660
It might also mean that we're only a few short
years away from Alien: Reboot.

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:32.960
Don't say it could never happen.

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:37.460
Hollywood rebooted RoboCop, Spider-Man, Evil
Dead, and plenty more.

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:40.430
There's no reason that Alien won't be reborn
soon enough, too.

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:43.680
After all, getting born — and reborn — is
kinda what Aliens do…

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:44.910
Thanks for watching!

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:47.310
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:49.350
Plus check out all this other cool stuff we
know you'll love too!

